WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.830 --> 00:00:04.710
When I think about taking a train,

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:07.730
I get this warm, fuzzy, nostalgic feeling,

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:09.152
sort of like--

00:00:09.152 --> 00:00:12.126
♪ There's something about a train ♪

00:00:12.126 --> 00:00:14.709
♪ That's magic ♪

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:17.630
Of course, the reality of train travel

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:20.610
in America is less than magical.

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:22.270
There's just huge parts of this country

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:25.150
where it's just a hassle to get anywhere by train,

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:26.450
and the trains barely run.

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:30.910
For the most part, the only passenger trains are Amtrak.

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:33.883
That's the case that it's been for almost 50 years now.

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:37.120
Even along the busy Northeast Corridor,

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:39.530
stretching from Washington, D.C. to Boston,

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:42.623
less than 10% of trips each year are taken by train.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:46.130
So it might be surprising
to learn that this guy,

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:47.950
a billionaire investor who co-owns

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:50.570
the Milwaukee Bucks basketball franchise,

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:52.360
is super optimistic about the future

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:55.010
of trains in America and has a plan

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:56.750
to bring them back.

00:00:56.750 --> 00:00:59.630
I think people lack imagination
a little bit sometimes,

00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:01.200
of what actually could happen.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:02.590
And once they can experience it,

00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:04.430
I think it'll be a question of, you know,

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:05.970
why is it not everyplace else?

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:19.266
So how did trains get
such a bad rap in America?

00:01:19.266 --> 00:01:22.510
Well, here's a little background.

00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:25.350
Just over a hundred years
ago, train travel was really

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:28.000
the only option for long-distance trips.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.880
But as two major technological
innovations came online,

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:35.360
passenger rail's competitiveness
dropped significantly.

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:36.860
One of those, of course, is the car.

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:38.670
And then to combine those vehicles

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:41.730
with a massive amount
of highway construction.

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:43.270
The other major technological innovation,

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:45.180
of course, was the airplane.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:47.440
Both of those were heavily subsidized

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:50.130
by the federal government,
and as a result,

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:54.310
the number of riders on passenger
trains started to plummet.

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:56.580
And so, the railroad
companies, they were losing

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:58.180
all sorts of money on
their passenger lines,

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:01.690
so they wanted to dump them,
so they basically convinced

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:04.760
the federal government
in 1971 to create Amtrak

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:08.090
and basically take over
intercity passenger rail.

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:09.860
In the busy Northeast Corridor,

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:13.370
Amtrak actually runs a fairly
popular, reliable service.

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:15.090
But in most of the rest of the country,

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:17.380
where Amtrak doesn't
even own its own tracks,

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:20.513
frequent delays are the norm,
and ticket sales are dismal.

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:24.270
But of course, it doesn't
have to be this way.

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:25.750
In much of Europe and Asia,

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:28.580
taking an intercity train
is as natural as it is

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:31.030
for Americans to hop into their own cars.

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:34.160
There's a dramatic and
large-scale public investment

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:37.860
in rail in East Asia and
Western and Central Europe.

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:39.950
That's just not an investment
we've made here in the U.S.

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:43.020
So we just have significantly
less service on offer.

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:45.070
But in some ways, you could
see that as a glass half-full,

00:02:45.070 --> 00:02:47.470
and there's a tremendous
amount of opportunity there.

00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:49.530
The question is, how
can we build more of it,

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:50.850
how can we afford that service,

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:52.910
and where is that service gonna work best?

00:03:00.816 --> 00:03:02.590
Brightline announcing
it's just a few weeks

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:04.180
away from service in Miami-Dade,

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:06.720
and they started testing the tracks today.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:08.580
Here in Miami, a city founded

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:11.520
by Gilded Age railroad
magnate Henry Flagler,

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:14.630
a new grand rail
experiment is taking place,

00:03:14.630 --> 00:03:16.570
the first brand new, privately-owned

00:03:16.570 --> 00:03:18.880
intercity passenger
rail service in America

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:21.320
in nearly a century.

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:24.000
It's the brainchild of
this guy, Wes Edens,

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.230
head of private equity firm
Fortress Investment Group.

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:29.270
How do you help convince a
culture that isn't really

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:31.860
interested in the train as transportation

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:34.290
to become interested in
trains as transportation?

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:35.390
Get 'em to try it once.

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:37.620
Not only is it the time
and the efficiency of it,

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:39.310
it's a very, very pleasurable experience.

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:41.060
It's a proven commodity around the world.

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:42.920
It's only the United States
in the industrialized world

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:45.830
that doesn't have it, but I
think that's gonna change soon.

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:49.220
Wes's project, called Brightline,

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:51.560
started operating in 2018,

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:54.100
running modern Siemens-built
passenger trains

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:56.900
between Miami, Fort
Lauderdale, and West Palm Beach

00:03:56.900 --> 00:03:59.590
at speeds at up to
around 70 miles per hour,

00:03:59.590 --> 00:04:01.230
running on existing freight track

00:04:01.230 --> 00:04:03.230
owned by the Florida East Coast Railway.

00:04:05.550 --> 00:04:08.860
By 2022, the route will
be extended up to Orlando,

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:11.080
on newly-built tracks
that will allow the trains

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:13.663
to cruise at up to 125 miles per hour.

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:17.770
The entire 250-mile route
will take around three hours,

00:04:17.770 --> 00:04:20.890
falling into an important
passenger rail sweet spot.

00:04:20.890 --> 00:04:23.403
It's too short to fly
but too long to drive.

00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:27.443
If it's too short,

00:04:27.443 --> 00:04:29.900
then rail service doesn't
really make much sense.

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:31.190
People are gonna take their car.

00:04:31.190 --> 00:04:32.850
And then you're talkin' 1,000 miles.

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:34.540
Aviation's always gonna be competitive.

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:37.260
But for that two to 500-mile range,

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:38.760
rail service really offers

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:41.110
potentially the best alternative.

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:44.000
These paired corridors
that are too far to drive,

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:47.830
too short to fly, there's
about a dozen significant ones.

00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:50.930
So Atlanta to Charlotte,
Dallas to Houston,

00:04:50.930 --> 00:04:53.570
Las Vegas to Los Angeles,
which is our next project.

00:04:53.570 --> 00:04:56.080
I think that it's quite
possible you'll see trains

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:58.553
in all those places
over the next 10 years.

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:06.800
I actually grew up in South Florida,

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:09.383
not far from where Brightline's
route passes today.

00:05:10.330 --> 00:05:12.410
My parents, like many other transplants,

00:05:12.410 --> 00:05:15.260
escaped cold, crowded New
York for the tropical,

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:17.163
laid-back charm of South Florida.

00:05:18.530 --> 00:05:19.850
And, like most of the U.S.,

00:05:19.850 --> 00:05:22.423
Florida has a
firmly-entrenched car culture.

00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:29.990
But in recent decades,
Florida's population has surged,

00:05:29.990 --> 00:05:32.560
as retiring baby boomers have
been lured in by the state's

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:35.130
lack of income and estate taxes.

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:37.970
Traffic, especially
along the I-95 corridor,

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:40.480
has become a nightmare at all hours.

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:42.690
Have you done the drive from
West Palm to Miami before?

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:43.960
I have.
What's that like?

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:45.480
It's terrible, you're stuck in at least

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:46.547
two to three hours of traffic.

00:05:46.547 --> 00:05:48.970
And just, Florida drivers--
Are terrible.

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:50.150
They're the worst.
They're just horrendous.

00:05:50.150 --> 00:05:52.120
They make New York drivers look like

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:53.984
they just got out of driving school.

00:05:53.984 --> 00:05:55.087
Well, a lot of them are New Yorkers

00:05:55.087 --> 00:05:56.363
who just made their way down here--

00:05:56.363 --> 00:05:57.541
That's true, that's true.
Brought their bad skills,

00:05:57.541 --> 00:05:59.310
and got worse.
And got worse as they drove

00:05:59.310 --> 00:06:00.740
down here.
That's so true.

00:06:00.740 --> 00:06:02.260
It's not hard to see how a train

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:04.610
would be an improvement over all of that.

00:06:04.610 --> 00:06:06.820
But logic aside, there's still the strong

00:06:06.820 --> 00:06:10.930
cultural bias against
trains, that bad reputation.

00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:12.750
So Brightline sweetens the deal,

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:16.220
and for about $18-40
each way, you get to ride

00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:18.910
on a bright, clean,
brand spankin' new train,

00:06:18.910 --> 00:06:20.943
with some luxury-flavored amenities.

00:06:29.480 --> 00:06:31.800
And finally, there's the
biggest luxury of all,

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:34.000
reducing your carbon footprint.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:36.270
We think we'll take four
million cars off the road here,

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:39.880
so that's millions of metric tons of CO2.

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:41.970
This system will run on biodiesel.

00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:44.040
Obviously, it's not fossil-free,

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:46.820
but you're concentrating
people into transportation,

00:06:46.820 --> 00:06:49.370
which is a lot more efficient
than individual cars.

00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:52.538
For all these potential upsides,

00:06:52.538 --> 00:06:55.253
Brightline has already faced
some serious challenges.

00:06:56.760 --> 00:06:59.440
One is a bizarrely high
level of fatalities

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:01.510
caused by collisions.

00:07:01.510 --> 00:07:03.590
A Brightline train comes
to a smashing stop,

00:07:03.590 --> 00:07:05.190
hitting a car on the tracks.

00:07:05.190 --> 00:07:06.810
Officials say that this person

00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:08.410
who was in this vehicle is okay.

00:07:09.930 --> 00:07:11.420
These accidents are pretty clearly

00:07:11.420 --> 00:07:12.980
not the railway's fault.

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:15.060
It's more like risky Floridian drivers

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:17.000
are used to ignoring crossing warnings

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:18.950
to get past slow-moving freight trains.

00:07:20.630 --> 00:07:22.440
To their credit, Brightline has undertaken

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:24.040
some measures to reduce these accidents,

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:26.240
such as improving signage at crossings,

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:28.993
and using drones to monitor
potential collisions.

00:07:30.340 --> 00:07:33.070
The real hurdle, though,
is the business itself.

00:07:33.070 --> 00:07:35.070
The total bill for Brightline's completed

00:07:35.070 --> 00:07:37.230
Orlando-to-Miami route is expected

00:07:37.230 --> 00:07:39.750
to come in at around $4 billion,

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:43.090
funded mostly by what are
called private activity bonds,

00:07:43.090 --> 00:07:47.070
a form of tax-exempt bonds
sold to private investors.

00:07:47.070 --> 00:07:49.320
Eventually, the bonds
will need to be repaid.

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:52.700
So far, ridership has been climbing.

00:07:52.700 --> 00:07:55.620
Last July, they welcomed
their one-millionth rider.

00:07:55.620 --> 00:07:57.720
But on a Tuesday afternoon, well,

00:07:57.720 --> 00:08:00.120
this is what our train looked like.

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:02.030
It's disconcerting to see how few

00:08:02.030 --> 00:08:03.480
people are on the train, though.

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:05.170
I walk my dogs every morning,

00:08:05.170 --> 00:08:07.320
and every time it goes by, I kinda look.

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:10.000
There's maybe three people riding it.

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:11.460
To help increase ridership,

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:13.680
they've partnered with
everyone from local museums

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:16.890
to ridesharing services to the Miami Heat.

00:08:16.890 --> 00:08:18.120
And more recently, they announced

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:20.420
a branding partnership with this guy.

00:08:20.420 --> 00:08:22.700
Hi, everybody!

00:08:22.700 --> 00:08:25.010
Which will result in
Brightline being renamed

00:08:25.010 --> 00:08:27.763
Virgin Trains USA sometime in 2020.

00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:31.703
But Brightline isn't just
betting on ridership.

00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:35.040
What train service does always really well

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:37.070
is it's not just making
money on the service,

00:08:37.070 --> 00:08:39.510
it's that knowing the
land around train stations

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:42.960
is incredibly valuable, and
Brightline's parent company

00:08:42.960 --> 00:08:44.760
effectively owns land in particular

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:46.623
around the station in central Miami.

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:50.260
We've built about 1,500,000
square feet of real estate.

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:53.970
I mean, this is a part of
Miami, that you would know well,

00:08:53.970 --> 00:08:57.080
was not really a vibrant economic zone.

00:08:57.080 --> 00:08:59.500
It now is really the hub of economic

00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:01.150
development here in downtown Miami,

00:09:01.150 --> 00:09:03.030
and transportation plays
a huge role in that.

00:09:03.030 --> 00:09:05.020
So we think that the
real estate part of it

00:09:05.020 --> 00:09:06.680
is a big component, and actually linking

00:09:06.680 --> 00:09:08.780
those two things together, economically,

00:09:08.780 --> 00:09:12.593
it creates a lot of the
foundation for the project.

00:09:14.200 --> 00:09:16.220
Brightline's Florida route isn't the only

00:09:16.220 --> 00:09:18.823
recent development for
passenger rail in the U.S.

00:09:20.960 --> 00:09:24.020
The company's next project is
a high-speed electric train,

00:09:24.020 --> 00:09:26.630
stretching from Las Vegas
to just outside L.A.,

00:09:26.630 --> 00:09:29.880
a route millions of
tourists drive every year.

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:32.400
Other high-speed rail
projects have been proposed

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:35.360
in places like Texas and
the Pacific Northwest,

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:37.640
though these proposals are
far behind Brightline's

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:41.440
operational service.

00:09:41.440 --> 00:09:43.570
Meanwhile, Amtrak isn't taking all of this

00:09:43.570 --> 00:09:46.100
private competition lying down.

00:09:46.100 --> 00:09:46.970
A couple of years ago,

00:09:46.970 --> 00:09:49.090
it brought on Richard Anderson as CEO,

00:09:49.090 --> 00:09:52.610
who previously revived Delta
Airlines from bankruptcy.

00:09:52.610 --> 00:09:55.550
We really need to reposition Amtrak

00:09:55.550 --> 00:09:59.520
as a modern mode of
transportation similar to what

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:02.770
we see in Europe and what we see in Japan.

00:10:02.770 --> 00:10:04.440
He's been cutting costs in an effort

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:06.810
to free up capital for
much-needed infrastructure

00:10:06.810 --> 00:10:09.170
improvements and revamped trains,

00:10:09.170 --> 00:10:11.300
like these state-of-the-art
high-speed cars

00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:13.200
that are coming in 2021

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:15.713
to the business-traveler-focused
Acela line.

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:19.230
While Amtrak still needs
subsidies to get by,

00:10:19.230 --> 00:10:21.260
it's financially healthier than ever,

00:10:21.260 --> 00:10:24.310
and in 2019, it set a
new record for ridership,

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:26.600
serving nearly a million more passengers

00:10:26.600 --> 00:10:27.623
than the year prior.

00:10:31.260 --> 00:10:33.950
Ultimately, the future of
American passenger rail,

00:10:33.950 --> 00:10:35.460
whether public or private,

00:10:35.460 --> 00:10:38.380
will depend on one really big question,

00:10:38.380 --> 00:10:41.210
will Americans want to
actually ride trains,

00:10:41.210 --> 00:10:42.660
or do we prefer to think of them

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:46.090
as nostalgia-inducing relics of the past?

00:10:46.090 --> 00:10:48.770
Rail has always made
sense, but it seems like

00:10:48.770 --> 00:10:51.323
it's never made more sense than in 2020!

00:10:52.250 --> 00:10:53.083
We're gonna need more of it.

00:10:53.083 --> 00:10:55.620
There's the climate issue,
the less people drive,

00:10:55.620 --> 00:10:58.670
the less people fly, the better
it is for the environment.

00:10:58.670 --> 00:11:00.330
Countries that invest in infrastructure

00:11:00.330 --> 00:11:01.950
are the ones that do the best,

00:11:01.950 --> 00:11:04.280
do the best economically,
do the best for the people,

00:11:04.280 --> 00:11:06.690
they create the best quality of life.

00:11:06.690 --> 00:11:09.360
We used to be the leader of
infrastructure in the world.

00:11:09.360 --> 00:11:11.610
And I mean, frankly, we're
not the leader anymore,

00:11:11.610 --> 00:11:13.503
and that's something that should change!

